=== 3D Chromatin-Conformation (EpiSwitch) Signature
<sec:episwitch-3d-genomic>

The EpiSwitch platform reads *3D chromosome architecture* — how DNA folds into loops and topologically associated domains inside the nucleus — rather than the linear methylation marks measured by standard arrays. This is a distinct epigenetic layer: the 3D genome reflects which enhancers and promoters are physically brought into contact, and hence which genes are actively regulated.

*Reported discrimination.* Hunter et al.\ (2025) profiled whole-blood 3D chromatin architecture in 47 severely affected (housebound) ME/CFS patients and 61 healthy controls @Hunter2025. A 200-marker chromosome-conformation panel distinguished the two groups with 92% sensitivity, 98% specificity, and 96% overall accuracy in the study's internal (retrospective) validation cohort. Pathway analysis of the implicated loci enriched for IL-2 signalling, innate immune activation, and JAK-STAT transduction, and for pathways also dysregulated in multiple sclerosis and rheumatoid arthritis.

*Severity applicability:* severe/very-severe — the cohort was housebound patients; applicability to mild/moderate ME/CFS is unknown.

#speculation(title: [3D Chromatin Architecture Is an Independent ME/CFS Biomarker Layer])[
  Three-dimensional chromosome architecture — measured as enhancer-promoter contacts and chromatin-loop structure in blood — differs between severe ME/CFS and healthy controls, forming a blood-based diagnostic signal that is orthogonal to (and potentially additive with) conventional DNA methylation and miRNA markers @Hunter2025. If replicated, this would give clinicians a fundamentally different measurement axis for ME/CFS — one based on gene-regulatory architecture rather than gene sequence or methylation marks.

  *Certainty:* 0.50 — single retrospective vendor-affiliated study (Oxford BioDynamics), no independent external validation cohort. The specificity (98%) was measured against healthy controls only; the test has not been assessed against fibromyalgia, depression, or long-COVID fatigue. Population relevance weight 1.00 (direct ME/CFS cohort).

  *Limitations:* Single study; commercial proprietary platform limits independent replication; severe/housebound cohort only; cross-condition specificity untested; and a high overfitting risk — 200 markers fitted in n=47 patients with only an internal validation split, so the reported 92%/98% figures are internal-validation numbers from a small cohort, likely to shrink in external replication. The severe/housebound cohort also differs from healthy controls on deconditioning, stress, polypharmacy, and sleep, so the signature may reflect being severely ill rather than ME/CFS-specific pathology. No replication exists as of 2026.

  *Falsifiability:* An independent cohort (n ≥ 100 ME/CFS vs matched controls) failing to reproduce the reported sensitivity/specificity within ±5 percentage points, or an EpiSwitch assay distinguishing ME/CFS from fibromyalgia with no better than chance accuracy, would refute the claim that this signature is specific to ME/CFS.

  *Consequence:* If this holds up in independent cohorts, researchers would gain a blood-based test that might one day confirm ME/CFS diagnosis objectively — but today it remains an unvalidated research finding, not a clinical test.
] <hyp:episwitch-3d-chromatin-biomarker>

#speculation(title: [The IL-2/JAK-STAT Axis Is Mechanistically Coupled to 3D Chromatin Architecture])[
  The biological plausibility of the IL-2/JAK-STAT pathway the EpiSwitch panel implicates is supported by non-vendor work showing that 3D chromatin topology is mechanistically coupled to IL-2 and JAK-STAT signalling: TRIM28 organises chromatin to control IL-2 production in T cells @Wei2025TRIM28chromatin; IL-2 signalling itself restructures the 3D genome of CD4+ T cells @Ward2025CD4threeD; and CTCF-coordinated 3D enhancer architecture shapes immune-related gene expression and is functionally coupled to JAK-STAT signalling (CTCF depletion rewires the CD4+ T-cell response to JAK inhibitors by reprogramming the STAT5 enhancer network) @Lee2025CTCFenhancer. These findings are not ME/CFS-specific but establish that the regulatory architecture EpiSwitch measures can plausibly participate in the immune dysregulation documented in ME/CFS. This IL-2 convergence is the same pathway underlying the low-dose IL-2 regulatory-T-cell hypothesis discussed in the treatment sections (see @spec:low-dose-il2-mecfs and the Low-Dose IL-2 entry in the pharmacodiagnostic compendium, Chapter @ch:mechanistic-cascade-tracing).

  *Certainty:* 0.45 — the 3D-chromatin↔IL-2/JAK-STAT coupling is well-supported in non-ME/CFS systems (human/mouse T cells) but its specific role in ME/CFS is inferred from the unvalidated EpiSwitch enrichment, not directly demonstrated.

  *Limitations:* The supporting evidence is non-ME/CFS-specific; translation to ME/CFS is inferred. No ME/CFS Hi-C study has directly confirmed the IL-2-responsive loop reorganisation at the implicated loci.

  *Falsifiability:* In an independent cohort, Hi-C or Capture-C in ME/CFS CD4+ T cells showing IL-2-responsive loop contacts at the STAT5 enhancer loci (Lee 2025) statistically indistinguishable (P ≥ 0.05) from healthy resting T cells after controlling for activation markers (CD69/CD25) would refute the mechanistic bridge.

  *Consequence:* If the IL-2/3D-chromatin link holds, it strengthens the case for IL-2 as a therapeutic target and links an epigenetic biomarker to a testable immune mechanism.
] <hyp:3d-chromatin-IL2-jakstat-axis>

#speculation(title: [A "Frozen" Chromatin State as Trapped Immune-Activation Memory])[
  The EpiSwitch panel enriches for IL-2/JAK-STAT pathway genes, but the platform measures a snapshot of loop architecture in blood cells. IL-2 stimulation alone restructures the CD4+ T-cell 3D genome, with dynamic topological changes at least as frequent as stable ones @Ward2025CD4threeD. The ME/CFS signature may therefore represent a loop conformation frozen in the "activated" position — the chromatin architectural residue of an immune-activation program that was triggered but never fully resolved. This reframes the finding from active IL-2 signalling (which serum-cytokine studies have not consistently confirmed) to "chromatin remembers activation," consistent with the ME/CFS paradox of normal-to-low circulating cytokines despite immune-dysfunction phenotypes.

  *Certainty:* 0.30 — mechanistically grounded (IL-2-driven 3D-genome reorganisation is documented) but the specific "frozen-in-activation" interpretation in ME/CFS is unvalidated and inferred. (Origin: brainstorm.)

  *Limitations:* No ME/CFS Hi-C/3C study has measured loop-conformation relaxation kinetics. The reframe resolves a paradox but is itself untested.

  *Falsifiability:* In ME/CFS CD4+ T cells, Hi-C at IL-2-pathway loci showing loop conformations indistinguishable from healthy cells after normalising for activation status (CD69/CD25), or loops that relax to baseline within hours of rest, would refute the frozen-chromatin hypothesis.

  *Consequence:* If ME/CFS immune cells are stuck in the "on" position at the level of DNA folding even after chemical signals fade, it would explain persistence after the trigger clears and point toward therapies that reset the folding rather than only dialling signals up or down.
] <hyp:frozen-chromatin-state>


*Competing approaches and research gap.* A competing blood-based approach — endogenous retrovirus (HERV) expression profiling — has reported perfect ME/CFS-versus-fibromyalgia group separation, a discrimination axis EpiSwitch has not yet tested @GimenezOrenga2025HERVsegregation. Because the leading diagnostic challenge in ME/CFS is distinguishing it from other fatiguing illnesses, demonstrating cross-condition specificity is the decisive next step for any candidate biomarker, including EpiSwitch.

#speculation(title: [TRIM28 as a Unifying Node Across 3D-Genomic, HERV, and IL-2 Findings])[
  Three independent lines converge on TRIM28 (KAP1): (1) TRIM28 organises chromatin loop-anchor regions and its loss disrupts IL-2 production in CD8+ T cells @Wei2025TRIM28chromatin; (2) ME/CFS HERV activation coincides with TRIM28/SETDB1 binding sites — the sites of the TRIM28/SETDB1 epigenetic silencing whose loss derepresses HERVs @GimenezOrenga2025HERVsegregation; and (3) IL-2/JAK-STAT is the pathway hub of the EpiSwitch 3D-genomic panel @Hunter2025. A parsimonious unifying hypothesis is that impaired TRIM28-mediated silencing/loop-maintenance simultaneously derepresses HERVs and destabilises the 3D-chromatin architecture that constrains IL-2/JAK-STAT loci — making the HERV signature and the EpiSwitch signature two consequences of one upstream chromatin-maintenance defect.

  *Certainty:* 0.30 — the three lines are individually supported but the convergence is inferred; the TRIM28 evidence is non-ME/CFS-specific (healthy/mouse T cells) and HERV data come from a small cohort. (Origin: brainstorm.)

  *Limitations:* No ME/CFS study has directly measured TRIM28 occupancy at the implicated loop-anchor or HERV loci. The convergence, though biologically plausible, is unverified.

  *Falsifiability:* ME/CFS immune cells showing normal TRIM28 occupancy and SETDB1-dependent H3K9me3 at the specific loop-anchor and HERV loci implicated by the EpiSwitch and HERV signatures would refute this unifying-node hypothesis.

  *Consequence:* If two otherwise-competing epigenetic fingerprints of ME/CFS trace back to a single chromatin-maintenance protein, the field's separate tests point at one upstream defect — a natural focus for mechanistic study and, eventually, a drug target.
] <hyp:trim28-unifying-node>

#synthesis(title: [Convergent Epigenetic Layers Point Toward a Shared Immune-Regulatory Defect])[
  Several independent epigenetic measurement layers — 3D chromosome-conformation (EpiSwitch) @hyp:episwitch-3d-chromatin-biomarker, the IL-2/JAK-STAT pathway enrichment it reports @hyp:3d-chromatin-IL2-jakstat-axis, endogenous-retrovirus expression, and conventional DNA-methylation and miRNA markers — each point toward a common theme of immune-regulatory dysregulation. The IL-2/JAK-STAT axis is the shared hub, and a single chromatin-maintenance mechanism (TRIM28/KAP1) plausibly underlies both the 3D-genomic and HERV signatures @hyp:trim28-unifying-node. The "frozen chromatin state" reframes this as a durable architectural memory of unresolved immune activation @hyp:frozen-chromatin-state.

  This convergence is important but must not be over-read: the EpiSwitch diagnostic itself is a single, vendor-affiliated, unreplicated study whose specificity was tested only against healthy controls, not against fibromyalgia, depression, or long-COVID fatigue. The convergent biology therefore strengthens a *mechanistic hypothesis* about shared immune dysregulation — not a validated clinical diagnostic. An independent cross-condition methylation study supplies an empirical analogue in a different epigenetic layer: part of the blood-epigenetic (methylation) signal separates patients from controls without separating ME/CFS from fibromyalgia @spec:methylation-shared-burden-axis, so a wholly generic-illness explanation for the EpiSwitch signature cannot yet be excluded @oq:episwitch-generic-illness-null. The decisive next steps are independent replication and a direct ME/CFS-versus-fibromyalgia comparison @oq:episwitch-cross-condition-discrimination.

  *Consequence:* Multiple independent epigenetic fingerprints of ME/CFS — even from "competing" tests — appear to be pointing at the same underlying immune-regulatory defect, which is encouraging for understanding the disease but does not yet justify using any of them as a clinical test.
] <syn:episwitch-epigenetic-convergence>

A 2026 follow-up by the same group extended this work from a single-condition diagnostic signature to a multi-condition *network-level* analysis across ME/CFS, Long COVID, post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), rheumatoid arthritis (RA), and multiple sclerosis (MS) @Hunter2026EpiSwitchCrossCondition. Rather than testing a new patient cohort, the study re-rendered existing genome-wide association study (GWAS) summary statistics for these five conditions as three-dimensional genomic anchors (via the proprietary EpiSwitch Orion platform), mapped these anchors to genes, and asked whether the resulting gene sets converge at the protein-interaction-network level despite sharing few individual genes. This analysis is hypothesis-generating only and is discussed below with its substantial evidentiary limits.

#speculation(title: [Cross-Condition Network Convergence of 3D-Genomic Architecture Is a Hypothesis, Not Demonstrated Shared Biology])[
  When GWAS loci for ME/CFS (DecodeME), Long COVID, PTSD, RA, and MS are re-rendered as three-dimensional genomic anchors and connected into protein-interaction networks, the resulting gene sets share few individual genes yet interconnect into a dense shared landscape with candidate hub genes (RUNX1, PPARGC1A/PGC-1$alpha$, STAT1, CDH2, NRP1, PLCG2) and enriched pathways (immune/cytokine signalling, interferon responses, mitochondrial biogenesis, metabolism, neuroendocrine processes) @Hunter2026EpiSwitchCrossCondition. This pattern — low gene-level overlap but apparent network-level convergence — is the central claim. It is offered as a unifying explanation for why clinically overlapping fatigue-associated syndromes (see Chapter @ch:comparative-nosology) share symptoms despite distinct triggering events.

  *Certainty:* 0.20 — the analysis is entirely in silico and computational-only (population-relevance weight 0.30 applied to a raw 0.35, then discounted for the substantial methodological caveats below). The constituent biological content it invokes (immune exhaustion, mitochondrial/metabolic dysregulation) is independently supported in ME/CFS and post-COVID (see @lim:cross-condition-convergence-evidence), but the specific cross-disease *convergence* claim has no independent (non-vendor) replication.

  *Limitations:* (1) The convergence is produced by the proprietary EpiSwitch/Orion 3D-anchor translation layer, which has no independent methodological benchmark. (2) The authors explicitly state no permutation-based statistical validation against a random null model was performed. (3) The hub genes are network-derived candidates that the authors state require independent experimental and clinical validation; the study's validation cohort tested only the earlier diagnostic classifier, not these hubs. (4) Raw GWAS loci for ME/CFS and Long COVID are essentially disjoint, so "convergence" is an interpretive re-reading, not an observed shared genetic architecture. (5) Severe/very-severe ME/CFS cohort from the prior study is reused as an input, so the network claim adds no new ME/CFS patient data and severity applicability is unknown.

  *Falsifiability:* An independent, non-proprietary re-analysis (e.g., using open-source 3D-genome maps or Hi-C-derived anchors, with permutation testing against a degree-matched null model) that fails to reproduce cross-condition network convergence beyond chance would refute the claim that the five conditions share a meaningful regulatory architecture.

  *Consequence:* If the convergence is real, it would mean different chronic-fatigue conditions share a common "wiring diagram" that a single network-level treatment might address — but the current evidence is too weak and too vendor-dependent to conclude this, so it remains a testable research hypothesis, not established biology.
] <hyp:cross-condition-3d-convergence>

#limitation(title: [Cross-Disease Convergence Claim Rests on Unvalidated, Vendor-Dependent Analysis])[
  The claim that ME/CFS, Long COVID, PTSD, RA, and MS share network-level regulatory architecture is not established biology. The supporting study is an in-silico analysis funded by, and largely authored by employees of, Oxford BioDynamics (11 of 14 authors), the maker of the proprietary EpiSwitch/Orion platform it uses; it performs no experimental validation, no permutation testing against a random null model, and its candidate hub genes (RUNX1, PPARGC1A, STAT1, CDH2, LAG3) have no independent replication as cross-disease drivers @Hunter2026EpiSwitchCrossCondition. Independent evidence does support the *constituent* biology the network invokes — CD8+ T-cell exhaustion in ME/CFS @Iu2024CD8Exhaustion, T-cell-exhaustion markers in post-COVID/ME-CFS @Shahbaz2026single-cell-immune, an energy-metabolism–immune-exhaustion interface @VanCampenhout2025EnergyImmune, and elevated soluble LAG-3 in persistent post-COVID symptoms @Ueland2025sLAG3postCovid — but these corroborate immune-exhaustion mechanisms *within* single diseases; they do not independently validate cross-disease network convergence. ME/CFS and Long COVID GWAS loci are essentially disjoint — the ME/CFS DecodeME genome-wide association study reports no loci shared with Long COVID (see @ach:decodeme-gwas) — a direct caution against over-reading network-based "shared biology" as shared genetics. Accordingly, the convergence model must be presented as a hypothesis for further testing — not as evidence that a single cross-disease diagnostic or treatment exists.

  A pharmacological caution sharpens this. The convergence groups ME/CFS with rheumatoid arthritis and multiple sclerosis as though they shared a druggable network — yet B-cell depletion, which is effective in those diseases, failed the ME/CFS Phase III trial @Fluge2019RituximabPhase3 (see @lim:rituximab-null-cannot-eliminate). A shared network that does not respond to the therapy that works in the conditions it is shared with is at least as plausibly a shared burden- or immune-activation layer as a shared disease mechanism — a circumstantial argument about the network claim rather than a decisive test of it, since rituximab's CD20 target sits off-axis from the IL-2 signalling the panel itself implicates.

  *Consequence:* Patients and clinicians should not interpret the EpiSwitch cross-condition finding as proof of a shared disease mechanism or a forthcoming unified test; its value is limited to generating testable network-level hypotheses.
] <lim:cross-condition-convergence-evidence>

*Post-publication critical appraisal.* The original EpiSwitch study has since drawn a formal critical appraisal in the same journal @Tirelli2026WheatAndChaff. Its strongest methodological objections are considered below; the appraisal itself is an opinion letter with no new data, and its authors advocate oxygen-ozone autohemotherapy for ME/CFS — a therapy they criticise the original authors for omitting — so its claims about reporting bias must be weighed against that competing interest.

#limitation(title: [The EpiSwitch Diagnostic Has a Published Methodological Critique and Remains Unreplicated])[
  Hunter et al. (2025) has still not been independently replicated, and its methods have been challenged in print @Tirelli2026WheatAndChaff. The critique is an opinion letter without new data, so it cannot by itself refute the finding; judged on merit its strongest objections divide into substantiated, inapplicable, and weak. *Substantiated:* the cohort is defined as "severe/housebound" without naming the diagnostic criteria used (Fukuda, Canadian Consensus, International Consensus, or IOM) and without a validated severity stratifier, so the sample cannot be reproduced from the paper; the classifier was tested only against healthy controls, so the possibility that it partly tracks general illness burden rather than ME/CFS-specific pathology was never probed; and the study's framing of rituximab-responsive conditions as a comparator is misleading, because the rituximab Phase III trial in ME/CFS was null @Fluge2019RituximabPhase3 and rituximab depletes CD20-positive B cells — a target off-axis from the IL-2 signalling the panel implicates. *Inapplicable to how the finding is presented here:* the charge of conflating statistical association with causal mechanism does not hold against a text that labels the result a biomarker association. *Weak:* the claim that the original authors selectively omitted earlier ME/CFS immunology does not survive checking (foundational cytokine and natural-killer-cell work is cited), and the demand to cite oxygen-ozone therapy is advocacy rather than a methodological requirement. Two confounds remain unaddressed on *both* sides of the dispute: neither the original study nor the critique controls for blood cell-type composition — the dominant technical confounder of any whole-blood epigenetic measurement, since a shift in leukocyte proportions can generate an apparent methylation or conformation difference with no change in any single cell's regulatory state — or for latent-virus status, medication, and smoking differences between groups. The interests are also asymmetric in opposite directions: the original authors hold a commercial stake in the platform, while the critics advocate a competing therapy, so no interest-free replication exists to adjudicate between them.

  *Consequence:* the first formal challenge to the EpiSwitch ME/CFS claim is itself an opinion piece with its own competing interest, so it does not refute the finding — but its verifiable points (an irreproducibly defined cohort, untested cross-condition specificity, and a null rituximab comparator) confirm that the test remains research-stage and must not be presented as a clinical diagnostic.

  *Severity applicability:* severe/very-severe — the criticised cohort was housebound patients; the critique does not extend the evidence to mild/moderate ME/CFS.
] <lim:episwitch-methodological-critique>

#open-question(title: [Does Any Blood Epigenetic Signature Distinguish ME/CFS from Fibromyalgia?])[
  The decisive unmet requirement for every candidate ME/CFS blood biomarker — EpiSwitch included — is discrimination from other fatiguing illnesses, and the EpiSwitch panel has never been tested against fibromyalgia. The first DNA-methylation study to make that comparison directly, an epigenome-wide association study of 188 women, found a two-part structure: one DNA-methylation axis separated ME/CFS from both fibromyalgia and controls and tracked post-exertional malaise and cognitive symptoms, while a second axis separated both patient groups from controls but not from each other and tracked general symptom burden @Polli2026MethylationAxes. This is direct evidence that a blood epigenetic signal in ME/CFS can carry *both* a disease-specific and a shared illness-burden component, and it is the reference against which any 3D-conformation panel must now be judged. Whether the EpiSwitch panel resembles the disease-specific axis, the shared-burden axis, or both is unknown; a competing review argues that conventional DNA methylation, not 3D genome architecture, is the tractable unifying readout for these overlapping conditions @Sharma2026MethylationUnifying.

  *Consequence:* before any blood test is offered as an ME/CFS diagnostic, it must be shown to separate ME/CFS from fibromyalgia, depression, and long-COVID fatigue — a step EpiSwitch has not taken, and which an independent methylation study shows is not automatic.

  *Severity applicability:* unknown — the EpiSwitch cohort was severe/very-severe (housebound), but the methylation comparison cohort was not stratified by severity.
] <oq:episwitch-cross-condition-discrimination>

#open-question(title: [Could the EpiSwitch Signature Be Entirely a Generic Marker of Chronic Illness?])[
  The cleanest null for the EpiSwitch finding is that the signature plays no causal or disease-specific role: it may be a generic marker of chronic illness that appears whenever any chronically-ill cohort is compared with healthy controls. Under that null, the pathway the panel reports (IL-2/JAK-STAT) would be a generic immune-activation readout rather than evidence about ME/CFS, and the cross-condition convergence with RA, MS, and PTSD would be the expected consequence of comparing chronic-illness cohorts rather than a shared disease architecture. This null is currently neither excluded nor confirmed. It is made more than hypothetical by an independent methylation study showing a real, symptom-burden-aligned axis that separates patients from controls without separating ME/CFS from fibromyalgia @Polli2026MethylationAxes, and — as indirect support only, since that trial tested a B-cell therapy rather than the panel itself — by the failure of B-cell depletion in the ME/CFS Phase III trial @Fluge2019RituximabPhase3. The only evidence against the null is non-ME/CFS T-cell and mouse work establishing that 3D chromatin topology *can* be coupled to IL-2/JAK-STAT signalling @Wei2025TRIM28chromatin @Ward2025CD4threeD @Lee2025CTCFenhancer — plausibility, not ME/CFS causation. If the null held, three claims in this document would need revision: the mechanistic bridge @hyp:3d-chromatin-IL2-jakstat-axis, the TRIM28 unifying node @hyp:trim28-unifying-node, and the cross-condition convergence @hyp:cross-condition-3d-convergence.

  *Consequence:* the possibility that the whole EpiSwitch signal is just "this person is chronically ill" has not been ruled out, so until an independent ME/CFS-versus-fibromyalgia comparison is done, the finding should not be used to argue for any specific ME/CFS mechanism.
] <oq:episwitch-generic-illness-null>
